<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for the Apple Watch app.

  NOTE TO ANYONE EDITING THIS COMMENT: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a
  comment. Using one here produces a malformed manifest, which fails the build
  or, worse, ships something App Store Connect refuses.

  The watch needs its OWN manifest. Apple: "For each executable or dynamic
  library in an app that uses a required reason API, the bundle that includes
  the executable or dynamic library needs to include a privacy manifest file
  that reports the API." The watch app is a separate bundle, so the phone's
  manifest does not cover it.

  SYSTEM BOOT TIME is declared because rep counting is built on it.
  CMLogItem.timestamp, which every accelerometer and device motion sample
  carries, is seconds since device boot. WatchMotionCapture subtracts the first
  sample's timestamp so everything downstream is an offset into the capture
  session, and SetDetector measures the time elapsed between samples to work out
  where a set begins and ends.

  Reason 35F9.1, verbatim from Apple's documentation:

    "Declare this reason to access the system boot time in order to measure the
     amount of time that has elapsed between events that occurred within the app
     or to perform calculations to enable timers. Information accessed for this
     reason, or any derived information, may not be sent off-device. There is an
     exception for information about the amount of time that has elapsed between
     events that occurred within the app, which may be sent off-device."

  That is exactly what we do, and the exception is what permits a detected set's
  duration to sync to the phone.

  NOT 8FFB.1. That reason is for turning boot time into ABSOLUTE timestamps for
  in-app events. We deliberately never do this: session relative offsets avoid
  the question entirely, and a test asserts DetectedSet.startedAt is an offset
  rather than a date. If anyone ever makes sample timestamps absolute, this file
  must change to 8FFB.1 in the same commit.

  The hard rule either way: the raw boot time, and anything derived from it
  other than elapsed durations, must never leave the watch.

  Everything else is empty, and checked:
  no file timestamp, disk space or active keyboard APIs; no UserDefaults, since
  settings arrive from the phone and are cached as JSON; no data collected, no
  network code, no tracking. Workouts are written to HealthKit on the user's own
  device, which is not third party collection.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
